        <div id="price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Is Orgain Protein Powder at Costco?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Costco does not publish a fixed national price for Orgain, and the tag varies by warehouse, region and whether a coupon is running. We could not find a dated Costco price for the current tub that we were willing to rely on, so rather than print a number that may be wrong at your store, this page gives you the arithmetic. The figures below are worked examples, not quotes.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rule is simple: multiply the warehouse tag by 0.044 to get the cost per 25 g of protein. That number comes from the label. The tub holds about 567 g of protein, and 25 divided by 567 is 0.044. So a $23 tub works out to about $1.01 per 25 g, a $30 tub to about $1.32 and a $40 tub to about $1.76. Cost per serving is simpler still: divide the tag by 27, which gives about $0.85 a scoop at $23 and about $1.48 at $40.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Tag price (example)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per serving (21 g)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g of protein</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">$23</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $0.85</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.01</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">$27</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.00</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.19</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">$30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.11</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.32</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">$35</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.54</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">$40</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.48</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about $1.76</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Members have reported much lower prices during promotions. A December 2022 Slickdeals post showed the 2.74 lb Pumpkin Spice tub at $22.97 for Costco members, and a December 2023 post reported $7.50 to $9 off Orgain powders and shakes in the warehouse and online. Orgain tends to appear in Costco's monthly savings books, so if the shelf price looks high, it is often worth waiting a few weeks for the next coupon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our broader <a href="/how-much-is-costco-protein-powder/">Costco protein powder price guide</a> puts Kirkland Signature Whey at about $0.70 to $0.80 per 25 g. Even at a coupon price, Orgain sits above that, and at full price it can cost more than twice as much per gram. That is not a flaw so much as the price of organic plant protein. If the premium surprises you, our explainer on <a href="/why-is-protein-powder-so-expensive/">why protein powder is so expensive</a> walks through where the money goes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-elsewhere" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Orgain Cheaper at Costco Than on Amazon or at Target?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Usually yes on a per-gram basis, because the Costco tub is bigger and warehouse pricing on large sizes tends to be sharp. On Amazon and at warehouse-club rivals, the standard 2.03 lb tub of the same powder has typically worked out to roughly $1.40 to $2.00 per 25 g of protein at the prices we tracked for our review. A Costco tub only beats that if its tag is below about $45, which, judging by the deal reports above, it usually is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Target and supermarkets mostly sell the 2.03 lb tub at full retail, so they tend to be the most expensive place to buy Orgain per gram unless a store promotion or circle offer is running. The advantage of those stores is flavor choice and the smaller size, which matters if you are trying a plant protein for the first time and are not sure you will finish 27 servings before the tub goes stale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sam's Club and BJ's also list Orgain in a 2.74 lb tub, so if you hold a membership at one of those clubs, compare tag prices before you drive. The math is identical: the same 0.044 multiplier works on any 2.74 lb tub of the 21 g formula. Our <a href="/sams-club-protein-powder/">Sam's Club protein powder guide</a> covers what that warehouse stocks.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more cost to count is the membership itself. Orgain alone will rarely pay for a Costco card. A member who buys one tub a month saves a few dollars a month against the small supermarket tub, judged per gram, so the saving is real but only worthwhile if you already shop there or also buy Kirkland whey, which is where Costco's protein pricing is strongest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Is in Costco's Orgain Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Per the label at the time of writing, a two-scoop, 46 g serving gives about 21 g of protein, roughly 150 calories and under 1 g of sugar, with a few grams of fiber depending on flavor. The protein comes from an organic blend of pea, brown rice and chia seed, and some newer labels add mung bean. It is vegan, gluten free and certified USDA Organic, and it has long been one of the few organic plant powders sold at a warehouse-club price.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweeteners are organic erythritol and organic stevia in most flavors, which is worth knowing if you are sensitive to sugar alcohols. Erythritol is usually tolerated better than other sugar alcohols, but a double serving delivers more of it, and some people notice bloating. The texture is thicker and creamier than a plain pea isolate because of added gums and a creamer base, which is a large part of why it is popular as a coffee and smoothie powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 21 g dose is the main weakness for people who train. Most muscle-building research uses about 25 to 40 g of protein a meal, and plant proteins tend to need the top of that range because they carry a little less leucine than whey. Hitting 30 g with this tub means about three scoops, which raises both the calories and the real cost per day by half.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Check Your Tub</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Flavors and formulas change</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Orgain has adjusted the blend and the sweetener on some flavors over time. Read the panel on the tub you actually buy, not a photo online, especially for allergens, sugar alcohols and serving size.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="lawsuit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Is the Costco Orgain Protein Powder Lawsuit About?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The case is Barton et al. v. Costco Wholesale Corporation, case number 2:26-cv-02364, filed on July 7, 2026 in the US District Court for the Western District of Washington in Seattle. Seven consumers, led by Stephen Barton, brought it as a proposed class action, represented by the law firm Hagens Berman. The defendant is Costco only. Orgain and its majority owner, Nestle Health Science, are not named as defendants in the coverage we read.
          </p>

          <p>
            The complaint, which runs to 87 pages, alleges that the Vanilla Bean and Creamy Chocolate Fudge flavors of Orgain Organic Protein Powder sold at Costco contain lead, cadmium and arsenic, and that Costco broke consumer protection laws by not disclosing it. The plaintiffs argue that the USDA Organic seal and marketing phrases such as "the power of clean" and "good, clean nutrition" led reasonable shoppers to assume the powder was free of harmful contaminants, and that Costco did not test for or disclose heavy metals.
          </p>

          <p>
            The evidence cited comes from three places, according to <a href="https://www.classaction.org/news/costco-class-action-lawsuit-alleges-orgain-organic-protein-powder-is-contaminated-with-heavy-metals" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">ClassAction.org's summary of the complaint</a>. It cites a January 2025 Clean Label Project report on about 160 protein powders, an October 2025 Consumer Reports investigation, and testing the plaintiffs' lawyers commissioned from an accredited lab. That lab testing reportedly found lead up to 67 parts per billion, cadmium up to 70.3 ppb and arsenic up to about 18 ppb across five samples of the two flavors. Newsweek reported the arsenic figure as 19.8 ppb.
          </p>

          <p>
            The plaintiffs seek to represent everyone in the US who bought those products from Costco for household use, with subclasses under the laws of Washington, California, Illinois, Minnesota, Ohio and Texas. They ask for damages and restitution and for an order that Costco disclose heavy metal levels, or stop selling the products until it does, per <a href="https://www.courthousenews.com/costco-accused-of-selling-protein-powder-contaminated-with-lead-arsenic/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Courthouse News Service</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What has Costco said?</h3>

          <p>
            Costco has filed a motion to dismiss the whole case with prejudice, as reported by <a href="https://topclassactions.com/lawsuit-settlements/lawsuit-news/costco-class-action-motion-claims-clean-nutrition-is-not-lead-free-promise/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">Top Class Actions on October 1, 2026</a>. It argues that no Costco statement ever promised the powder was free of heavy metals, that phrases like "clean" are vague marketing puffery, and that the highest lead reading the plaintiffs allege, 3.37 micrograms per serving, is less than half the FDA's interim reference level of 8.8 micrograms a day. Costco accepts that five samples appeared to exceed California's Proposition 65 threshold but argues that figure is a warning level with a wide safety margin, not a measure of harm.
          </p>

          <p>
            As of our last check in late November 2026, we found no ruling on that motion, no settlement and no recall. If a settlement is announced, Costco members who bought these flavors would normally be told how to claim through a settlement website, and we will update this page if that happens. Until then, everything above is an allegation or an argument, not a finding.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Does Orgain Protein Powder Have Lead in It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, measurable lead has been reported in Orgain's plant powder, as in most plant protein powders. Consumer Reports' October 2025 testing put Orgain Organic Plant-Based Vanilla Bean at about 143% of its daily level of concern for lead per serving and labeled it fine to have occasionally. Its level of concern is 0.5 micrograms a day, borrowed from California's Proposition 65, which is deliberately cautious. You can read <a href="https://www.consumerreports.org/health/supplements/protein-powders-and-shakes-contain-high-levels-of-lead-a4206364640/" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">the Consumer Reports results here</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two benchmarks in this story are far apart, which is why both sides can point to the same numbers. By the Proposition 65 yardstick, one serving a day of the Vanilla Bean flavor is over the limit. By the FDA's interim reference level, which Costco cites, even the highest alleged serving is under half the daily figure. Neither number says the powder is poisonous; they disagree on how much margin is enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lead in plant protein comes mostly from soil, and organic farming rules govern pesticides and fertilizers, not heavy metals. That is the gap the lawsuit is built on. It also means switching to another organic plant powder does not automatically fix the problem. Whey and egg proteins tend to test lower on average, and our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists the products with the best published results.
          </p>

          <p>
            Practical guidance follows from that. For most adults, an occasional Orgain shake is a small exposure. People who use protein powder twice a day, pregnant readers, and parents giving it to children have the most reason to choose a lower-lead powder or ask a doctor first. Our page on <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> puts the heavy-metals question in context with the other real risks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Should You Buy Orgain Protein Powder at Costco?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy it if you want an organic, dairy-free powder for smoothies, oatmeal or coffee, you use it a few times a week, and you like the taste. At a coupon price it is one of the cheapest ways to get a USDA Organic plant protein, and the bigger tub lowers the per-gram cost compared with the 2.03 lb supermarket size.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if your priority is cheap protein per gram, a high dose for muscle building or the lowest possible heavy-metal exposure. Kirkland whey costs far less per 25 g and gives 25 g in one scoop. A 30 g plant powder suits lifters better. And readers worried about lead should look at a whey isolate or a plant powder with strong published test results.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already have a tub, there is no recall and no court finding that tells you to throw it out. Use it as you normally would, or switch if the lawsuit has changed how you feel about it. Keep the receipt or your Costco order history in case a settlement is announced, because claim forms often ask for proof of purchase or at least the approximate date.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the full list of what your warehouse stocks and how each ranks on testing and cost per gram, see our <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/">best Costco protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

