        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tag prices instead of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The Orgain tub looks like half the price of a Kirkland whey bag, but it holds about a third as much protein. Multiply the Orgain tag by 0.044 and compare that with Kirkland's roughly $0.70 to $0.80 per 25 g before deciding which is the bargain for your budget.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming USDA Organic means tested for metals</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The organic seal covers how the crops were grown and processed. It says nothing about lead, cadmium or arsenic, which come mostly from soil. That gap is the core of the lawsuit, so if heavy metals worry you, look for published test results rather than an organic badge.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the lawsuit as a recall</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A class action complaint is a set of allegations, and Costco has asked the court to dismiss it. There has been no recall and no court finding about safety that we could find. Make your decision on the published test data and your own use, not on a headline.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using two scoops for a muscle-building dose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two scoops give only 21 g of protein, below what most muscle research uses per meal, and plant protein works best at the top of that range. Lifters usually need about three scoops, which raises calories and the daily cost by half. A 30 g formula can be cheaper per dose.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a giant tub of an untried flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twenty-seven servings is a lot to finish if you dislike stevia or the thick texture. Seasonal flavors rotate at Costco, and returns of opened food vary by warehouse. If you have never had Orgain, try a small tub or single-serve packet from a supermarket before committing to the warehouse size.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much is Orgain protein powder at Costco?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It varies by warehouse and promotion, and Costco does not publish one national price. Deal reports have shown the 2.74 lb tub as low as $22.97 for members in 2022, with $7.50 to $9 coupons in later years. To compare fairly, multiply your tag by 0.044 for the cost per 25 g of protein, or divide it by 27 for the cost per serving.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many servings are in the Costco Orgain tub?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About 27. The 2.74 lb tub holds roughly 1,243 g of powder, and the label serving is two scoops, or 46 g, giving about 21 g of protein. That is around 567 g of protein per tub. If you use three scoops to reach a 30 g dose, the same tub lasts about 18 servings.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is there a lawsuit against Costco over Orgain protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Barton et al. v. Costco Wholesale Corporation was filed on July 7, 2026 in federal court in Seattle. Seven consumers allege Costco failed to disclose lead, cadmium and arsenic in the Vanilla Bean and Creamy Chocolate Fudge flavors. Costco has moved to dismiss, and as of late November 2026 we found no ruling or settlement.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Was Orgain protein powder recalled at Costco?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  We found no recall of Orgain protein powder at Costco or elsewhere. The lawsuit asks the court to make Costco disclose heavy metal levels or stop selling the products until it does, but that is a request in a civil case, not an FDA or company recall. Check Costco's recalls page for any change.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Orgain protein powder have lead?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Testing has found measurable lead. Consumer Reports' October 2025 results put Orgain Vanilla Bean at about 143% of its cautious 0.5 microgram daily level of concern per serving and rated it fine to have occasionally. Costco argues the highest alleged reading is under half the FDA's 8.8 microgram interim reference level. Pregnant readers should ask a doctor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Orgain at Costco the same as Orgain at Target?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The formula is the same Organic Protein Plant-Based Powder, at 21 g of protein per two-scoop serving. The difference is size: Costco sells a 2.74 lb tub, while Target and most supermarkets sell 2.03 lb. Costco also stocks fewer flavors at a time and occasionally a Superfoods version or a seasonal flavor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Orgain a good protein powder to buy at Costco?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is a good choice for occasional smoothie and coffee use if you want organic, dairy-free protein and like the taste. It is not the best value per gram at Costco, where Kirkland whey costs far less per 25 g, and its 21 g dose is low for lifting. Readers focused on heavy metals may prefer a whey isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
